      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A heifer recovering from a rumenotomy is a delicate situation, and it's important to keep a keen eye on her. In the days following surgery, watch for signs of internal infections or abscesses, which can be a bit like spotting a hidden treasure map in a pile of old scrolls. Red flags to look out for include a sudden drop in appetite, a noticeable change in behavior, or signs of discomfort when touched. Keep an eye out for any unusual discharge or swelling, as these could be the first hints of trouble brewing in the depths of her belly.\n\nAs for reintroducing her diet, think of it as a carefully planned feast for a royal banquet. Start with small, frequent meals of high-quality forage, gradually increasing the amount over the next few weeks. This will help her rumen adjust without causing undue stress. Avoid sudden changes in diet, as that can be like throwing a wrench into a well-oiled machine. It's also wise to keep her hydrated and monitor her weight closely, as these are good indicators of her overall health and recovery.\n\nRemember, recovery is a journey, not a sprint, and taking it one step at a time will help ensure your heifer makes a full and healthy recovery. Keep an eye",
      "source": "target",
